WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.701
The new climate crisis warning.

00:00:01.701 --> 00:00:03.336
A study says the Arctic

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:04.537
could be free of sea

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:06.206
ice during the month of September,

00:00:06.206 --> 00:00:08.008
about a decade earlier

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:09.275
than previous projections.

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:10.443
And this would have dire

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:12.312
ripple effects right around the world.

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:13.713
The scientists findings are published

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:15.515
in the journal Nature Communications.

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:17.384
They want the first September one

00:00:17.384 --> 00:00:18.718
report to completely melt

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:19.486
the Arctic Sea

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:21.554
ice may happen by the 2030

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:23.456
that's earlier than anticipated.

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:24.290
And this could happen

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.292
even if the world made significant cuts

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:29.396
right now to planet heating pollution.

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:30.130
Our chief

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:31.398
climate correspondent, Bill Weir,

00:00:31.398 --> 00:00:32.832
joins me now from New York.

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:35.769
And Bill, so anoxic with no sea ice.

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:36.703
What would that mean?

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:39.639
How would that impact us all?

00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:41.541
It would have so many impacts.

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:42.809
It's kind of important

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.010
to think about the Arctic

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.212
as the immune system for planet Earth.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:47.313
And if it goes,

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:48.581
it makes all of our problems

00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:50.417
get much worse, much faster.

00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:50.950
We've known

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.052
it's been heating up there

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:54.421
four times faster than most of the globe.

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:55.555
20, 21.

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:57.057
It rained at the highest peak

00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:58.691
in Greenland for the first time.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:00:59.192
Last year.

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:00.126
A hurricane

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:02.162
managed to push warm air up there

00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:03.963
and created this late melting pattern.

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:05.131
Now, looking at the numbers

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:05.732
in the satellite

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:06.433
that it looks like

00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:06.900
it's losing

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.236
about 12 to 13% of sea ice per decade

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:11.704
in September there.

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:13.339
And I think we have animation from NASA

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:15.341
that just shows a timelapse

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:16.910
from satellite images

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.913
of just how dramatically it's gone away.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.981
And the main factor here

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:24.084
is that all that white ice

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:25.652
is reflecting sunlight

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:27.854
and deflecting all of the sun's energy

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:31.591
there that open water dark water absorbs

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:32.258
kind of the way

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:34.327
a black dashboard in a car

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:36.863
would at a much higher rate.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.766
And so it is yet another reminder.

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:41.067
Now they look at the models.

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:42.068
Even the

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:44.504
IPCC's latest state of the science report

00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:45.138
that just came out

00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:46.539
predicted middle of the century

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.741
before we'd see an ice free Arctic.

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:52.345
And now to says this new examination

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:53.813
looks at the old climate models

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:54.380
and it looks like

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.381
they were just conservative.

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:56.716
And it's going to happen

00:01:56.716 --> 00:01:58.551
more dramatically than we thought.

00:01:58.551 --> 00:01:59.152
And so

00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:00.420
but ten years earlier,

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.055
that is a significant difference

00:02:02.055 --> 00:02:03.356
from what we've been expecting.

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:05.792
So why is it happening so quickly then?

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:08.161
It is

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:10.296
the combination of just unchecked

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:11.965
planet cooking, pollution, just building

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:12.565
up, building up.

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:13.233
It is more

00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:14.834
and more of the reflective

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:15.535
ice going away.

00:02:15.535 --> 00:02:17.070
So it's heating up faster.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.671
It's also ocean currents

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:20.206
and atmospheric things

00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:21.474
wobbles in the jet stream

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:23.109
that we're just now understanding

00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:25.078
because they're brand new to science.

00:02:25.078 --> 00:02:26.846
And so you hear a lot of this surprise,

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:28.515
but this will also change

00:02:28.515 --> 00:02:30.450
human behavior the way it always does.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.084
People, shipping

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:31.718
companies will now

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:33.253
see the Northwest Passage

00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:34.854
as a more viable route

00:02:34.854 --> 00:02:35.955
instead of going south

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:38.057
to get around the world

00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:40.126
some see profit in a melting Arctic,

00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:42.061
even as it seems to be a huge

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:43.830
red flag to scientists.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.100
And if we then make significant cuts

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:48.334
to fossil fuels,

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:49.769
the conversation that you have had

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:51.070
and I have spoken at great length

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.906
about will that change anything?

00:02:52.906 --> 00:02:54.374
Could this turn this around?

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:55.441
Could it delay it

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:57.911
Yes.

00:02:57.911 --> 00:02:59.612
I mean, that's the one variable

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:00.213
that's the one

00:03:00.213 --> 00:03:02.882
lever humanity has is stopping

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.518
the rate of that pollution emission.

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:06.252
Right. Now.

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:07.253
And, you know,

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:09.222
the argument is from some as well.

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:11.391
We may be past certain tipping points.

00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:12.392
Why bother

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:14.093
or it's too big of a challenge.

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:15.461
Every 10th of a degree

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:16.529
could be the difference

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:17.864
between the landscape

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:19.866
you grew up on, living or dying.

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:21.301
It could be the difference between

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:24.037
coral reefs existing or not entire,

00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:25.505
you know, species

00:03:25.505 --> 00:03:26.272
or green belts

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:28.274
and all the things that go with that

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:30.109
in terms of peace and prosperity.

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:32.412
So, yeah, that is the that's the lever

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:34.881
to grab

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:35.815
What it shows

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:36.950
is that we're definitely behind

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:37.517
the curve, right?

00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:38.351
We're definitely behind.

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:39.285
We need to be doing more.

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:40.753
Bill, we're always great to see you.

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:41.354
Thanks, Bill.

